# zentone: BT.2390 row kernel — 4.01x faster, bit-identical
date:  2026-07-28
host:  Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
build: release, NO -C target-cpu=native
input: 3840-px RGB f32 row, source_peak 4.0 -> target_peak 1.0

## The gap

`ToneMapCurve::map_row` already dispatched SIMD strip kernels for Reinhard,
ExtendedReinhard, ReinhardJodie, TunedReinhard, Narkowicz, Hable, AcesAp1 and
Agx. **Bt2390 was the only curve with no row path** — it fell through the match
to the default per-pixel `map_rgb` loop.

That is exactly what the ARM measurement showed and what pointed here:

  agx_default   14.11us   3.33x NEON-vs-scalar   (has agx_row)
  hable_filmic   2.94us   1.63x                  (has hable_row)
  narkowicz      3.95us   1.22x                  (has narkowicz_row)
  bt2390        26.49us   0.99x   <- most expensive curve, ZERO SIMD benefit

A curve that is both the slowest and gains nothing from SIMD is the signature of
one that never reaches a SIMD kernel at all.

Per channel the old path recomputed
`ks = (1.5 * target_peak / source_peak - 0.5).clamp(0.0, 1.0)` — a divide, an
FMA and a clamp — despite `ks` depending only on the two curve parameters, and
branched on `e1 < ks`, which blocks vectorisation. It also re-matched the curve
enum on every pixel.

## The fix

`bt2390_3_tier` / `bt2390_4_tier`, `#[archmage::magetypes(define(f32x8),
v4(cfg(avx512)), v3, neon, wasm128, scalar)]`, following the shape the other
curve kernels already use. `ks`, the reciprocals and the output scale are
hoisted into a `Bt2390Params` computed once per row; the knee becomes a
`f32x8::blend` select instead of a branch. RGBA preserves alpha the same way
`narkowicz_4_tier` does.

## Result

  before  26.49us
  after    6.61us      4.01x

BIT-IDENTICAL to the per-pixel `map_rgb` reference — 0 differing lanes at
w=3840/1920/641/7, both RGB and RGBA (w=7 is below one f32x8, exercising the
pure-tail path). The NEON and scalar tiers are also bit-identical to each other
(0 differing lanes, same widths), so the knee select introduces no tier
divergence. 138 lib tests green.

## Measurement note

An A/B that timed "NEON enabled" then "NEON disabled" in one process read
9.47us / 6.06us, suggesting the scalar tier was faster. A clean single-run
measurement of the shipped path reads 6.61us — i.e. the first-arm reading was
inflated by measurement order, the same bias that produced a false 6%
"regression" in linear-srgb earlier the same day. The 4.01x above is from the
single-run measurement; no aarch64 tier special-case was added, because the
apparent tier difference was not real.

## Follow-up: ACES AP1 checked with the same reasoning — already optimal

bt2390's signature (most expensive curve + 1.00x NEON-vs-scalar) was worth
checking against the other 1.00x curve, `aces_ap1`. Its tier looked identical in
kind: `aces_3_tier(_token, ...)` ignores the token and loops the scalar
per-pixel `aces_ap1`.

An SOA (planar) kernel was written for it — r/g/b gathered into f32x8 vectors,
both 3x3 matrices and all three rational polynomials vectorised, operation
order preserved exactly. It came out BIT-IDENTICAL to the per-pixel reference
(0 differing lanes at w=3840/1920/641/7/3, RGB and RGBA) and delivered NOTHING:

  before 6.18us     after 6.11us     (same harness, same run)

1.1%, i.e. noise. Reverted.

The existing code comment was correct and this measurement confirms it:

  // ACES is a cross-channel matrix → per-pixel; the tier's target_feature
  // context still lets LLVM auto-vectorize the rational polynomial.

The 1.00x NEON-vs-scalar reading does NOT mean "no SIMD here". On AArch64 NEON
is baseline, so the forced-scalar arm autovectorises too — 1.00x means the two
arms compile to equivalent code, which for an already-autovectorised kernel is
the expected result, not a gap. bt2390 was different because it had no row
kernel at all and fell through to the per-pixel `map_rgb` path, paying an enum
match and a recomputed `ks` per channel.

So the 1.00x signature is necessary but not sufficient: check whether a row
kernel EXISTS before concluding there is headroom. zentone's curves are now
accounted for — agx 3.33x, hable 1.63x, narkowicz 1.22x (all with working
kernels), bt2390 fixed 4.01x, aces_ap1 already optimal.
